Discord Messenger for IRIX (Dear ImGui)
=======================================

A Discord-compatible messenger for IRIX 6.5 that looks like Discord's own
client: the same program as the macOS, Linux and Windows versions, drawn
with Dear ImGui on IRIX's OpenGL.  From Discord Messenger
(https://github.com/DiscordMessenger/dm).  The Motif version (product
dmessenger) is lighter and suits every SGI; this one wants fast OpenGL.

Run:     /usr/local/bin/discord-messenger-imgui
Needs:   IRIX 6.5.22 or later, X11 and OpenGL (GLX 1.2, OpenGL 1.1);
         any MIPS CPU.  Every library it uses besides IRIX's is linked into
         the program.
Speed:   it draws the whole window with textured triangles, so it is
         smooth where OpenGL textures in hardware (High and Maximum
         IMPACT, Octane MXI, VPro) and slow where IRIX textures in software
         (Indy XL and XZ, Indigo2 Solid IMPACT, Octane SI): a few frames a
         second while scrolling there; idle it uses no CPU.  On those
         machines the Motif version is the better choice.

Logging in: with a token, the "authorization" header of any request a
logged-in discord.com page makes (a browser's developer tools, Network).
It is kept in ~/.discordmessenger/settings.json, readable only by you, and
shared with the Motif version; you can put it there yourself:
{"Token":"..."}.  (The QR code login is switched off for now: Discord
demands a captcha at its end that IRIX cannot show.  DM_QR_LOGIN=1 brings
it back.)

Note: third-party clients are against Discord's terms of service.  The
risk of a ban is low, but it is there, and it is yours.

What it shows: servers, channels, the member list and messages, with
Discord's formatting, replies, reactions, embeds, pictures, avatars and
colour emoji.  A click on a reaction adds or takes back yours; the right
button on a message offers Add Reaction, Reply and, on your own, Edit and
Delete; the face in the message box opens the emoji picker; a click on a
picture shows it large.  The gear by your name has the member list, the
text size, the theme (dark, light or several others) and the notification
sound (sfplay and the desktop's sound scheme; DM_SOUND names another file).

Try it without logging in: discord-messenger-imgui --demo shows sample
servers, channels and conversations to click through (#stress-test has two
thousand messages, for scrolling speed).  --bench [frames] scrolls the demo
and prints how long each frame took; DM_DEMO_CHANNEL=stress-test with it
measures the big channel.

Files:   ~/.discordmessenger: settings.json, imgui.conf (text size, theme,
         panes) and cache/: pictures (DM_CACHE_MB) and messages/
         (DM_HISTORY_MB; DM_NO_HISTORY=1 keeps none).  Log Out empties both.
         /usr/local/lib/discord-messenger-imgui: fonts/ (DejaVu, Noto Color
         Emoji; DM_FONT_DIR names another directory), cacert.pem (the roots
         of trust; DM_CA_FILE names another file), licenses/.
Links:   open in the browser that DM_BROWSER or BROWSER names (web links
         only: http and https).
OpenGL:  DM_GL_INFO=1 prints the OpenGL in use; DM_GL_ALPHA_TEST=1 draws
         without blending (rougher text edges; for boards that blend slowly).

Source, and how it was built: https://github.com/atomchild411/dm
(irix/dist has this package's recipe).

Licenses: licenses/ -- Discord Messenger is MIT licensed.  Linked in:
Dear ImGui (MIT), OpenSSL (Apache License 2.0), LLVM's libc++ (Apache
License 2.0 with LLVM exception), FreeType (FreeType License), libpng (PNG
Reference Library licence), zlib, bzip2 (BSD-style), libwebp (BSD, with
Google's patent grant), JSON for Modern C++ and Nayuki's QR Code generator
(MIT).  The fonts: DejaVu (Bitstream Vera licence, and public domain
changes) and Noto Color Emoji (SIL Open Font License 1.1).  cacert.pem is
from Mozilla (MPL 2.0).
